--- prove.sh ---
     1	#!/usr/bin/env bash
     2	set -euo pipefail
     3	
     4	python3 py2mpy.py solution.py > solution.mpy
     5	python3 py2mpy.py concrete-tests.py > concrete-tests.mpy
     6	
     7	kompile reference-semantics/semantics.k \
     8	  --backend llvm \
     9	  --main-module MPY-KRUN \
    10	  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \
    11	  --output-definition runtime-kompiled
    12	
    13	krun solution.mpy \
    14	  --definition runtime-kompiled \
    15	  --output pretty
    16	
    17	krun concrete-tests.mpy \
    18	  --definition runtime-kompiled \
    19	  --output pretty
    20	
    21	kompile verification.k \
    22	  --backend haskell \
    23	  --main-module VERIFICATION \
    24	  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \
    25	  -I . \
    26	  --output-definition verification-kompiled
    27	
    28	# Prove the reusable loop invariant independently.
    29	kprove spec.k \
    30	  --definition verification-kompiled \
    31	  --spec-module SPEC \
    32	  --claims SPEC.loop-correct \
    33	  --output pretty
    34	
    35	# Use the independently proved invariant as the loop lemma for the entry
    36	# theorem.  Both labels must remain selected so the claim is available.
    37	kprove spec.k \
    38	  --definition verification-kompiled \
    39	  --spec-module SPEC \
    40	  --claims SPEC.loop-correct,SPEC.sort-even-correct \
    41	  --trusted SPEC.loop-correct \
    42	  --output pretty
--- solution.mpy ---
     1	Module(
     2	  FuncDef("sort_even", Params("l"),
     3	    Assign(
     4	      Name("evens"),
     5	      Call(
     6	        Name("sorted"),
     7	        Subscript(Name("l"), Slice(NoBound, NoBound, Int(2)))))
     8	    Assign(
     9	      Name("odds"),
    10	      Subscript(Name("l"), Slice(Int(1), NoBound, Int(2))))
    11	    Assign(Name("result"), ListExpr())
    12	    Assign(Name("i"), Int(0))
    13	    Assign(Name("odd"), Int(0))
    14	    For(Name("odd"), Name("odds"),
    15	      Expr(
    16	        Call(
    17	          Attribute(Name("result"), "append"),
    18	          Subscript(Name("evens"), Name("i"))))
    19	      Expr(Call(Attribute(Name("result"), "append"), Name("odd")))
    20	      AugAssign(Name("i"), "+", Int(1)))
    21	    Return(
    22	      BinOp(
    23	        "+",
    24	        Name("result"),
    25	        Subscript(
    26	          Name("evens"),
    27	          Slice(Call(Name("len"), Name("odds")), NoBound, NoBound))))))
--- solution.py ---
     1	def sort_even(l: list):
     2	    evens = sorted(l[::2])
     3	    odds = l[1::2]
     4	    result = []
     5	    i = 0
     6	    odd = 0
     7	    for odd in odds:
     8	        result.append(evens[i])
     9	        result.append(odd)
    10	        i += 1
    11	    return result + evens[len(odds):]

codex
The classification boundary is now clear. The verification module contains 11 definitional equations/recurrences, 3 operational rules, and 3 unproved simplification facts. The simplification facts are trusted domain lemmas—not proved derived lemmas—because `prove.sh` compiles them into `VERIFICATION` before either proof. The separately proved `loop-correct` claim is real Stage 1 evidence, but it is a `spec.k` claim rather than an inventoried verification rule.
